---
import TagCanvas from '../components/TagCanvas.svelte';
import TagBorderland from '../components/TagBorderland.svelte';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>

  <body>
    <Header />
<div class="page-heading">
  <TagCanvas client:load />

  <div class="header-overlay">
    <div class="essay-heading">
      <h1>Tumultuous Tags</h1>
      <p>Written by Alysa Meng and Maryelizabeth Koepele</p>
      <p>The background is comprised of reading tags, brightness scaled linearly based on frequency.</p>
      <p><em>Scroll down for more!</em></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<main>
    <div class="essay-content">
      <h3>Introduction</h3>
      <p>
       The tags in Voca seem quite simple; find an interesting subject or location and the tag will lead you to relevant poems. As our 
       exploration of the tags demonstrate, this data is more layered than it seems.
      </p>

      <h3>A Typology of Reading Tags</h3>
      <p>One of the first things we noticed about the tags is that some poetry readings have tags associated with individual poems, while others only have tags associated with the reading as a whole.
      This disparity is a result of inconsistent grant funding for the Poetry Center’s digitization projects.
      </p>
      <p>The tags themselves consist of thousands of unique words and phrases which are associated with the recordings. We scraped these from the site and put them into Google Sheets to enable our data 
      exploration. Looking at the thousands of words in our spreadsheet, we felt both overwhelmed in anticipation of trying to coax meaning from so much data and excited by the actual language in the list. 
      Voca is an archive of poetry, and many of the tags reflect that subject matter with terms like <a href="inside/outside worlds">https://voca.arizona.edu/items-with-tag/5965/5965</a>,’ 
      <a href="ultratranslation">https://voca.arizona.edu/items-with-tag/4835/4835</a>, <a href="non-existence">https://voca.arizona.edu/items-with-tag/6409/6409</a>’ and many others that seem to reflect a taggers’ response to the reading 
      more than any sort of search optimization.
      </p>
.</p>

      <h3>Why explore digital tags?
      <p>The data from institutional digital archives such as Voca may surprise users with its evidence of human creation. Despite a clean, alphabetized digital interface, the tags contain language that resists standardization 
      and that reflects individual experiences with the recordings. For example, the tags ‘surreal’ and ‘surrealism’ both appear. An automated tagger would likely have collapsed these into one term. The existence of both serves 
      as evidence of the multiple taggers who made slightly different associations with similar content.
      </p>
      <p>This lack of standardization is an issue that is frequently present in digital collections and digital archives today. As early as 2009, in their foundational textbook, Arlene G. Taylor and Daniel N. Joudrey were discussing 
      in which digital environments it is most appropriate to use a controlled vocabulary, noting that "people do not think of the same terms to express the same aboutness concepts." (333) Taylor and Joudrey discuss the benefits and drawbacks 
       of using controlled vocabularies compared to social tagging practices. A controlled vocabulary is more likely to result in a concise, manageable list of subject terms, while social tagging practices are more likely to capture nuances and 
       intra-community understandings that controlled vocabularies might miss.
       </p>
       <p>Voca's archive, however, is an excellent example of a digital archive which has been processed and tagged solely by library staff members and still contains broad variation in tags. Both the variations of actual words used and the 
       type of data that seems to have been prioritized in the tags varies between readings. The poetry center has been building Voca for x years; this sort of variation reflects the changes in staff, workflows, and understandings of digital data over time.
       </p>
       <p>This kind of messy data is common in digital archives today, but it may soon be seen as a product of its time. As AI tools become more routinely used in library work, the future of digital tagging practices like Vocas's grow even more uncertain.
       </p>
       <p>Standardized digital tagging systems offer many benefits to digital archives. They create data that is more user-friendly, more search-engine friendly, and likely in smaller amounts. The purpose of this essay is not to argue for the creation 
       and use of purposefully messy data. Instead, we are curious to discover the narratives contained in this kind of jointly created metadata.
       </p>

       <h3>Categorizing tags</h3>

      <p>Putting tags into categories is difficult.</p>
      <TagBorderland client:visible />

      <!-- bonus content on track tags -->
      <h3>Reading vs. Track Tags</h3>
      <div class="track-content">
        <p>
          Like readings, Voca contains track tags. This provides a more granular breakdown of topics
          in a reading.
        </p>

        <p>Placeholder most common reading tags</p>
        <p>Placeholder most common track tags</p>
        <p>Placeholder most common co-occuring reading-track tags</p>

        <p>
          These breakdowns suggest that the track tags may serve a different purpose than the
          reading tags. Future analysis can be done to understand the landscape of tags for tracks,
          as well as the dialogue between reading and track tags.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Missing Tags</h3>
      <div class="missing-tags-content">
        <p>
          There is another layer of complexity to these tags looking at which readings and tracks
          are tagged in the first place.
        </p>

        <p>Placeholder for number of tags histograms.</p>

        <p>
          This makes things unsearchable. Analyzing the tags as a proxy for understand the themes in
          the poems and readings doesn't work due to incomplete metadata.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Tag Analysis Takeaways</h3>
      <div class="conclusion-content">
        <p>Maryelizabeth come up with brilliant concluding thoughts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    </main>

    <Footer />

  </body>
</html>
<style>

